X86_64 DekkerBroken
(* A "lock" built from plain stores and loads, Dekker/Peterson style
   without a fence: each thread raises its own flag, then reads the other
   thread's flag, and enters the critical section only if that flag is
   down (if it is up, the thread backs off and never enters, to keep the
   example finite). Each thread records in `edx` whether it entered; the
   critical section does a non-atomic increment of `counter` and then
   lowers the flag.
   This is the store-buffering (SB) pattern: on x86-TSO each thread's
   flag store may sit in its store buffer while its load reads the other
   flag's initial 0, so both threads enter at the same time. A violation
   is expected; this is the one weak behaviour TSO has.
   Registers: rsi = own flag, rdi = other thread's flag.
   Property: no final state in which both threads entered and an
   increment was lost (counter = 1). Both entering with counter = 2 is
   legitimate: the second entered after the first lowered its flag.
   A lost update is only possible when both threads are in the critical
   section at once (the flag release drains behind the counter store).
# flags: -fuel 300 -witness
# expect: Sometimes
# Proved reachable: Kraken.Promising.Reach.DekkerBroken.dekker_broken_reachable (Kraken/Promising/ReachX64DekkerBroken.lean)
*)
{
flag0=0; flag1=0; counter=0;
0:rsi=flag0; 0:rdi=flag1;
1:rsi=flag1; 1:rdi=flag0;
}
threads { P0: worker done; P1: worker done; }
code {
worker:
  xorl %edx,%edx
  movl $1,(%rsi)
  movl (%rdi),%eax
  testl %eax,%eax
  jne done
  movl $1,%edx
  movl (counter),%ecx
  addl $1,%ecx
  movl %ecx,(counter)
  movl $0,(%rsi)
  jmp done
done:
}
~exists (0:edx=1 /\ 1:edx=1 /\ [counter]=1)
